1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,960
Today we'll be talking
about imitation learning.

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:09,940
Great.

4
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:11,780
So as a partial
recap from last time,

5
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:14,200
we started talking about
some of the notation

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,480
that we use for
representing behavior

7
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:21,240
and formulating problems as
a decision-making problem.

8
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:23,540
We talks about states
and observations

9
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:25,640
where we either have
access to something that

10
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:28,840
can fairly, completely represent
the state of the world,

11
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,560
or a more partial
representation that we will

12
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:34,120
refer to as an observation.

13
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:39,760
We also will be representing
actions or decisions with at,

14
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,920
and then we can think
about a trajectory

15
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:49,397
as an example of behavior
that we've taken over time.

16
00:00:49,397 --> 00:00:51,980
We also introduced this notion
of a reward function, which is,

17
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:55,920
how good is a given
state and action.

18
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:00,180
And lastly, this
notion of a policy

19
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:03,455
which is either conditioned
on the current state, what

20
00:01:03,455 --> 00:01:04,580
actions do we want to take.

21
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,800
Or conditioned on a
history of observations,

22
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:08,100
what actions should we take.

23
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:10,420
And usually this
policy is the behavior

24
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:11,590
that we're trying to learn.

25
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:14,620

26
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:18,300
We briefly mentioned how this
policy can be represented

27
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:19,440
using a generative model.

28
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:22,910
And we'll actually touch on
that a little bit more today.

29
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:24,660
And the last thing
that's not on the slide

30
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:27,660
is that we talked about how the
goal of reinforcement learning

31
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:32,260
problems is usually to maximize
the expected sum of rewards.

32
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:35,220
And today we'll be
talking about one way

33
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:39,325
to do that by trying to
imitate a good policy.

34
00:01:39,325 --> 00:01:40,700
And in particular,
the plan today

35
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,660
is to go over some of the
basics for imitation learning.

36
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:46,660
Then we'll talk about
learning expressive policy

37
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:49,940
distributions, and why that's
important for imitation

38
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:51,020
learning.

39
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:53,600
Then we'll talk about learning
from online interventions.

40
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:55,433
And time permitting,
we'll talk a little bit

41
00:01:55,433 --> 00:01:57,420
about how to collect
demonstrations

42
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:59,890
in the first place.

43
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:01,390
So some of the key
learning goals.

44
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:04,090
This is, in some
ways, orthogonal

45
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:05,430
to the main topic of the class.

46
00:02:05,430 --> 00:02:07,755
But it's actually very
important to understand in order

47
00:02:07,755 --> 00:02:09,630
to get performant
imitation learning systems.

48
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:12,050
So we'll be talking about how
to represent distributions

49
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:16,050
using neural networks rather
than deterministic functions.

50
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:19,170
We'll also be talking about why
expressive distributions matter

51
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:20,850
for imitation learning.

52
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:23,170
And one challenge
that can come up

53
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:25,610
with imitation learning,
which is compounding errors

54
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:29,290
and how to address
compounding errors.

55
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:30,150
Great.

56
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:33,083
So what is the goal
of imitation learning?

57
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:34,750
Like the goal of
reinforcement learning,

58
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:36,910
we want to try to
achieve high reward.

59
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:41,090
We want to get a good policy
that can do the task well.

60
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:43,850
And the way that we'll
try to do that is we'll

61
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:46,170
assume that we're given
some trajectories that

62
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:48,050
are collected by an expert.

63
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:49,730
This is our data.

64
00:02:49,730 --> 00:02:52,990
And we'll refer to these
trajectories as demonstrations.

65
00:02:52,990 --> 00:02:56,590
So it's a special trajectory
that is demonstrating

66
00:02:56,590 --> 00:02:59,230
how to perform the task.

67
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:03,590
And we'll use D to denote
these demonstrations.

68
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:06,910
A trajectory, it'll be a
sequence of states and actions.

69
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:10,150
And as an example of what
this demonstration data might

70
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:15,430
look like, if we wanted to learn
how to autonomously drive a car,

71
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:16,950
we could have
demonstrations that

72
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:19,512
are in the form of
people driving cars.

73
00:03:19,512 --> 00:03:21,470
Because people are pretty
good at driving cars,

74
00:03:21,470 --> 00:03:25,550
most of the time,
and so we could

75
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:27,650
record from the
sensors on a car,

76
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:31,030
as well as record steering
commands, acceleration,

77
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:32,110
and so forth.

78
00:03:32,110 --> 00:03:35,490
And using a data set of
human drivers driving,

79
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:39,790
we could then try to mimic that
in order to train an autonomous

80
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:42,830
driving agent.

81
00:03:42,830 --> 00:03:47,430
Now, we don't know
exactly how humans drive.

82
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:50,350
We don't know the
policy that humans

83
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:52,550
are using in order to drive.

84
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:54,940
So we can think about
this demonstration

85
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:58,390
data set as being sampled from
some unknown policy pi expert.

86
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:01,028

87
00:04:01,028 --> 00:04:02,820
And then the goal is
to learn a policy that

88
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:07,140
performs at the level or similar
to the level of the expert

89
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:11,020
policy by mimicking what's
present in the demonstration

90
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:12,200
data set.

91
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:12,700
Cool.

92
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:15,780
So this is the problem setup.

93
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:23,100
Now, let's think about version 0
of how we could try to do this.

94
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:28,220
So a very simple way that
we could do this is first,

95
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:34,780
we're given our
demonstration data set.

96
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:40,090
So this is some data set that
contains state action pairs.

97
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:42,962

98
00:04:42,962 --> 00:04:44,420
These are each of
the state actions

99
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:47,580
and each of the
demonstration trajectories.

100
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:52,520
And then we could say that
we want to train some policy

101
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:55,880
to mimic the demonstration.

102
00:04:55,880 --> 00:05:01,280
So we would be
training some policy.

103
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,360
And in particular, we could
think about the actions

104
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:07,240
that this policy
is going to take.

105
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:11,080
So if we were to take one of
the states in our demonstrations

106
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:15,480
and run it through our
policy, then we would get some

107
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,960
predicted action, a hat.

108
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,720
So let's say that this is
sampled from pi theta given

109
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:26,320
a state in the demonstration.

110
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:27,960
And then we could
try to basically say

111
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:30,320
we want this predicted
action to be as close as

112
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:33,480
possible to the action
that the demonstrator took.

113
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:39,400
So we could then
minimize the difference

114
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:44,920
between the predicted action
and the action in the data set.

115
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,160
And we could do
this minimization

116
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:51,270
over all of the states and
actions in our demonstration

117
00:05:51,270 --> 00:05:52,550
data set.

118
00:05:52,550 --> 00:05:55,510
Once you train a policy such
that the predicted actions are

119
00:05:55,510 --> 00:05:58,590
as close as possible to the
actions in your data set,

120
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:02,190
then you can just
deploy your policy.

121
00:06:02,190 --> 00:06:07,590
This is a policy that
should be mimicking

122
00:06:07,590 --> 00:06:09,870
the actions in your data set.

123
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:12,430
This is version 0 of
imitation learning.

124
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:15,550
So given demonstrations
collected by an expert,

125
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:18,990
you essentially do
supervised regression

126
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:21,070
to the expert actions.

127
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:23,570
If your policy is
a neural network,

128
00:06:23,570 --> 00:06:28,030
this means doing a forward
pass, sample a mini batch.

129
00:06:28,030 --> 00:06:31,150
From here, do a forward
pass on your neural network

130
00:06:31,150 --> 00:06:34,170
to get the predicted
actions, compute the loss,

131
00:06:34,170 --> 00:06:36,790
and then back propagate the
loss into the parameters

132
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:37,890
of the neural network.

133
00:06:37,890 --> 00:06:40,810
And you do that iteratively
with your favorite optimizer,

134
00:06:40,810 --> 00:06:42,830
some form of stochastic
gradient descent

135
00:06:42,830 --> 00:06:46,017
in order to optimize
your policy.

136
00:06:46,017 --> 00:06:48,100
So it's essentially a form
of supervised learning.

137
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:51,770

138
00:06:51,770 --> 00:06:55,670
And then from here, you can
then deploy your learned policy.

139
00:06:55,670 --> 00:06:58,170
And it should be able
to mimic the expert

140
00:06:58,170 --> 00:07:01,330
and do something sensible.

141
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:04,730
So in this case,
we're considering

142
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:06,410
a deterministic policy.

143
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:09,250
And we're just essentially
doing L2 regression.

144
00:07:09,250 --> 00:07:12,690
I guess here I wrote it
as a stochastic policy.

145
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:15,610
But let's correct
that and let's have

146
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:19,890
this be a deterministic policy.

147
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:22,730
The expert might not
be deterministic,

148
00:07:22,730 --> 00:07:25,272
and that might lead
to some issues.

149
00:07:25,272 --> 00:07:27,230
So let's actually talk
about that a little bit.

150
00:07:27,230 --> 00:07:30,450
So what could go wrong?

151
00:07:30,450 --> 00:07:32,150
Let's go back to
our driving example.

152
00:07:32,150 --> 00:07:34,530
So we have a data set
from human drivers.

153
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:38,030
And we recorded the states
or the observations.

154
00:07:38,030 --> 00:07:41,010
We recorded the steering
commands, and any other actions

155
00:07:41,010 --> 00:07:43,250
that we might want to use.

156
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:48,722
And let's say that for this
particular car, in some cases,

157
00:07:48,722 --> 00:07:51,950
some human drivers might
decide to go straight

158
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:54,850
and other drivers might
decide to merge left,

159
00:07:54,850 --> 00:07:58,830
maybe because they want
to move to a faster lane.

160
00:07:58,830 --> 00:08:01,990
So our data set might
contain both examples

161
00:08:01,990 --> 00:08:04,450
of going straight in
situations like this,

162
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:09,870
and examples of merging left
in situations like this.

163
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:15,070
Now, a question for you.

164
00:08:15,070 --> 00:08:18,230
If you train a policy
with L2 regression

165
00:08:18,230 --> 00:08:22,470
on a data set that
has these examples,

166
00:08:22,470 --> 00:08:25,350
what would a policy that was
trained with L2 regression

167
00:08:25,350 --> 00:08:29,550
do in a situation like this?

168
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:32,510
So the policy might go to
the middle of the two lane.

169
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:33,809
And let's see why that is.

170
00:08:33,809 --> 00:08:38,590

171
00:08:38,590 --> 00:08:46,220
In this example, say we
have the steering command.

172
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:48,220
And if we thought
about the distribution

173
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:52,580
over actions that are covered
in the data set for drivers that

174
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:56,940
chose to go straight, their
distribution of actions

175
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:59,980
would look maybe something
like this, where this

176
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,720
is like 0 steering command.

177
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:03,820
This is staying straight.

178
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:09,220
And for the drivers that
chose to merge left,

179
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:12,100
their distribution over actions
for the steering command

180
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:16,580
might look something like this.

181
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:19,490
So this is the
probability of actions.

182
00:09:19,490 --> 00:09:23,310

183
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:25,180
These are the two examples.

184
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:28,740
If they're deciding
to merge left,

185
00:09:28,740 --> 00:09:31,160
they maybe have a steering
command of negative 2.

186
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:32,577
This is if you
stay straight, that

187
00:09:32,577 --> 00:09:34,340
might have a steering
command of 0.

188
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:39,740
And then in your data set,
the overarching distribution

189
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:42,860
over actions represented
in the data set

190
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:48,040
might look something
like this, where

191
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:51,980
maybe, more likely people are to
go straight than to merge left.

192
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:54,560
But you get this
multimodal distribution

193
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:57,720
that has two peaks, one at going
straight and one at merging

194
00:09:57,720 --> 00:09:59,880
left.

195
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,120
And then when you
use L2 regression

196
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:06,240
to try to fit this data,
what it's going to do

197
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:07,740
is it's going to
predict the mean.

198
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:09,448
So it's going to
predict the mean action.

199
00:10:09,448 --> 00:10:11,040
And in this case,
the mean action

200
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:13,960
would be something like here.

201
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:16,560
And that would be probably
not very good action

202
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:19,720
to take because
that might straddle

203
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:24,160
the lines on the highway.

204
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:26,360
And also we can see that
this action actually

205
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:30,060
has very low probability
under the data distribution.

206
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:34,400
So it's also objectively or
mathematically a poor choice

207
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:39,320
because it had low probability
under what the data was doing.

208
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:42,810
So L2 regression is the equation
from the previous slide.

209
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:44,670
So basically
minimizing this squared

210
00:10:44,670 --> 00:10:48,270
loss between the predicted
action and the true action.

211
00:10:48,270 --> 00:10:50,310
And then the other
question was, what

212
00:10:50,310 --> 00:10:53,270
is the difference between
the green and the red here?

213
00:10:53,270 --> 00:10:55,670
So essentially what I'm
drawing in the green

214
00:10:55,670 --> 00:11:01,210
is this is the distribution
over expert actions.

215
00:11:01,210 --> 00:11:04,390
Maybe I'll use expert
to refer to that,

216
00:11:04,390 --> 00:11:08,460
conditioned on only the data
that chose to go straight.

217
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:13,190

218
00:11:13,190 --> 00:11:19,430
The blue is the distribution
over expert actions

219
00:11:19,430 --> 00:11:22,310
if they choose to merge left.

220
00:11:22,310 --> 00:11:26,150
And then the red
is the distribution

221
00:11:26,150 --> 00:11:33,380
of actions represented in the
data set for a given state.

222
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:36,030

223
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:38,092
The red is actually what
your data looks like.

224
00:11:38,092 --> 00:11:39,550
And this is a gap
here just showing

225
00:11:39,550 --> 00:11:41,008
this is what the
distribution looks

226
00:11:41,008 --> 00:11:46,370
like for one subset of drivers
and the other subset of drivers.

227
00:11:46,370 --> 00:11:49,290
You're asking, can you
sample from the data set

228
00:11:49,290 --> 00:11:52,290
and do what was in the
data set, basically?

229
00:11:52,290 --> 00:11:52,845
Yeah.

230
00:11:52,845 --> 00:11:54,970
So the tricky thing with
sampling from the data set

231
00:11:54,970 --> 00:11:56,428
and doing what's
in the data set is

232
00:11:56,428 --> 00:11:59,410
that, that would look
like a nearest neighbors

233
00:11:59,410 --> 00:12:02,310
approach within the realm
of machine learning.

234
00:12:02,310 --> 00:12:05,410
And it is, in some
ways, a viable approach

235
00:12:05,410 --> 00:12:07,270
to a number of machine
learning problems.

236
00:12:07,270 --> 00:12:09,350
But one big challenge
is that ultimately,

237
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:13,410
when the car is driving or when
you have some autonomous driving

238
00:12:13,410 --> 00:12:15,090
agent, it's going
to face scenarios

239
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:17,290
that are different from
what's in the data set.

240
00:12:17,290 --> 00:12:23,730
And when it's in those
different positions,

241
00:12:23,730 --> 00:12:25,970
you won't necessarily have
examples in your data set

242
00:12:25,970 --> 00:12:30,330
that you can just pick from.

243
00:12:30,330 --> 00:12:32,570
So you won't necessarily
be able to say like, oh, I

244
00:12:32,570 --> 00:12:34,710
saw what the expert did
in exactly this situation

245
00:12:34,710 --> 00:12:37,530
because, ultimately, you'll
be evaluating scenarios

246
00:12:37,530 --> 00:12:38,760
that are different.

247
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:43,760
So what would you do if the
actions are not numerical?

248
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:48,440
In a slide or two, we'll talk
about discrete actions as well.

249
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:51,040
So to go over this
example on the slides,

250
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:53,940
we can say that the data for
staying straight is in yellow.

251
00:12:53,940 --> 00:12:57,000
The data for merging
left is in blue.

252
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,440
The combined data set, maybe
because more people decided

253
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:05,160
to stay straight, is shown
in the black dotted line.

254
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:11,440
And then if we train a model
to minimize the L2 error,

255
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:14,600
then the deterministic
policy will

256
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:17,220
choose to take an action
that's right here,

257
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:20,520
which is the mean of the
black distribution, which has

258
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:22,830
low probability under the data.

259
00:13:22,830 --> 00:13:25,305

260
00:13:25,305 --> 00:13:27,680
Now, you might be wondering,
how often does this actually

261
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:28,472
happen in practice?

262
00:13:28,472 --> 00:13:31,024

263
00:13:31,024 --> 00:13:34,600
Maybe you might think that this
is a contrived example where

264
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:37,580
there is variability in
what people would do.

265
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:41,140
It turns out that this action
actually happens all the time.

266
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:44,480
People and demonstrators often
do things in different ways,

267
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:47,820
not just in driving and not
just in key decision states.

268
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:50,260
And this happens,
especially when

269
00:13:50,260 --> 00:13:52,940
data is collected
by multiple people

270
00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:54,860
because people have
different preferences.

271
00:13:54,860 --> 00:13:57,360
They have underlying ways that
they like about doing things,

272
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:59,260
and you can't
directly observe all

273
00:13:59,260 --> 00:14:01,580
of those preferences
and all the things

274
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:04,100
that might affect
their behavior.

275
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:06,380
And so as a result,
there are some scenarios

276
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:10,580
where if you have one expert
policy that's always takes

277
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:14,260
a consistent strategy, it's OK
to use a deterministic policy.

278
00:14:14,260 --> 00:14:17,380
But in many other cases,
it's actually very important

279
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:20,420
to make sure that you account
for this multimodality

280
00:14:20,420 --> 00:14:22,620
in the data.

281
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:26,020
And so the natural question
that arises from here

282
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:28,740
is, instead of just
learning the mean,

283
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:30,900
could we actually
learn to represent

284
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:34,180
more of the distribution
of the expert data

285
00:14:34,180 --> 00:14:35,950
and not just the mean?

286
00:14:35,950 --> 00:14:40,630

287
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:43,090
And so this is where
learning distributions

288
00:14:43,090 --> 00:14:45,550
with neural networks comes in.

289
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:50,410

290
00:14:50,410 --> 00:14:55,550
Let's start a new whiteboard.

291
00:14:55,550 --> 00:14:59,890
So in the case of the
deterministic policy,

292
00:14:59,890 --> 00:15:03,770
we had some neural network.

293
00:15:03,770 --> 00:15:07,650
I will use a box to
represent a neural network.

294
00:15:07,650 --> 00:15:11,350
And we're taking as
input some state,

295
00:15:11,350 --> 00:15:14,050
and we're outputting
the mean action,

296
00:15:14,050 --> 00:15:16,110
the mean of a distribution.

297
00:15:16,110 --> 00:15:18,090
And so because it's only
representing the mean

298
00:15:18,090 --> 00:15:20,570
it's hardly a
distribution at all.

299
00:15:20,570 --> 00:15:23,330
And instead what we'd
like to be able to do

300
00:15:23,330 --> 00:15:26,010
is have this neural
network actually

301
00:15:26,010 --> 00:15:36,710
output the parameters of a
distribution over action.

302
00:15:36,710 --> 00:15:42,430
And if it can output a
distribution over actions,

303
00:15:42,430 --> 00:15:45,530
then we should be able to
capture more than just the mean.

304
00:15:45,530 --> 00:15:48,530

305
00:15:48,530 --> 00:15:49,030
Cool.

306
00:15:49,030 --> 00:15:52,893
So this is often the way that
we try to learn distributions

307
00:15:52,893 --> 00:15:55,310
with neural networks, is we
actually output the parameters

308
00:15:55,310 --> 00:15:56,830
of those distributions.

309
00:15:56,830 --> 00:15:58,270
So you can think
about it as this.

310
00:15:58,270 --> 00:16:00,390
You have a neural network
outputs parameters.

311
00:16:00,390 --> 00:16:03,610
If the distribution class that
you choose is very expressive,

312
00:16:03,610 --> 00:16:06,630
then you should be able to
get expressive distribution

313
00:16:06,630 --> 00:16:09,750
as output.

314
00:16:09,750 --> 00:16:11,710
Now, let's think about
a couple examples

315
00:16:11,710 --> 00:16:13,130
of what this might look like.

316
00:16:13,130 --> 00:16:15,790
So if your actions
are not continuous

317
00:16:15,790 --> 00:16:19,750
and instead they are
discrete actions,

318
00:16:19,750 --> 00:16:22,110
maybe you're trying
to train a policy

319
00:16:22,110 --> 00:16:25,350
to play a video game
like Super Mario,

320
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:26,830
then the actions
might be something

321
00:16:26,830 --> 00:16:30,370
like up arrow, down arrow,
press A, press B, right arrow,

322
00:16:30,370 --> 00:16:33,210
something like that.

323
00:16:33,210 --> 00:16:35,250
What the neural
network could output

324
00:16:35,250 --> 00:16:38,450
is basically this categorical
distribution over actions

325
00:16:38,450 --> 00:16:42,810
where, in this
discrete case, it will

326
00:16:42,810 --> 00:16:45,248
output the probability
of the first action,

327
00:16:45,248 --> 00:16:46,790
the probability of
the second action,

328
00:16:46,790 --> 00:16:50,450
the probability of the
third action, and so forth.

329
00:16:50,450 --> 00:16:55,010
And so you can think of this
as a classification problem.

330
00:16:55,010 --> 00:17:00,050
And this distribution is
actually maximally expressive.

331
00:17:00,050 --> 00:17:03,530
And what I mean by that is that
there isn't any distribution

332
00:17:03,530 --> 00:17:08,250
over actions that this cannot
represent because it's a fairly

333
00:17:08,250 --> 00:17:14,290
simple categorical or pretty
simple set of discrete possible

334
00:17:14,290 --> 00:17:15,817
options.

335
00:17:15,817 --> 00:17:17,150
So this is actually really nice.

336
00:17:17,150 --> 00:17:19,609
We can get a really expressive
distribution this way.

337
00:17:19,609 --> 00:17:21,329
Unfortunately, a
lot of the world

338
00:17:21,329 --> 00:17:24,990
is not just one dimensional
discrete action choices.

339
00:17:24,990 --> 00:17:27,089
We often have much
more complex actions,

340
00:17:27,089 --> 00:17:30,918
and that's why we need to think
about how to model distributions

341
00:17:30,918 --> 00:17:31,710
over those actions.

342
00:17:31,710 --> 00:17:35,320

343
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:39,710
So if we move towards continuous
actions like in a driving

344
00:17:39,710 --> 00:17:41,460
scenario where you
have steering commands,

345
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:45,560
for example, the most
natural thing to do

346
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:47,260
is to use a Gaussian
distribution.

347
00:17:47,260 --> 00:17:48,927
We talked about
outputting just the mean

348
00:17:48,927 --> 00:17:52,120
of a Gaussian distribution,
which corresponds to minimizing

349
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:54,320
the squared error.

350
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:58,520
But you can also have the neural
network output the variance.

351
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:01,140
And fortunately, if you
have it output the variance,

352
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:05,240
it's not going to do anything
particularly helpful.

353
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:09,040
In this example, it'll just
output a wider variance Gaussian

354
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:12,760
in order to try to cover
more probability mass.

355
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:14,280
And it's not very expressive.

356
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:17,640
And so we want to be able to
use distribution classes that

357
00:18:17,640 --> 00:18:20,720
are more expressive
so that we can

358
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:25,640
try to ultimately get actions
that are high probability.

359
00:18:25,640 --> 00:18:30,220
So the question was, if we have
some action distribution that

360
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:34,260
looks like this, for
example, would we be happy

361
00:18:34,260 --> 00:18:36,483
if we can perfectly model this?

362
00:18:36,483 --> 00:18:37,900
Or would that still
lead to issues

363
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:39,817
where you might have an
indecisive policy that

364
00:18:39,817 --> 00:18:43,646
chooses one mode and then
chooses another mode?

365
00:18:43,646 --> 00:18:47,740
The answer here is that
if you have a good state

366
00:18:47,740 --> 00:18:50,460
representation, then
you should be happy.

367
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:53,600
And in particular, say you
modeled this perfectly,

368
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:55,560
and for example,
you chose like this,

369
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:57,985
and then you start
merging left--

370
00:18:57,985 --> 00:18:59,860
if you know that you've
started to merge left

371
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:01,820
and your velocity
is moving left,

372
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:04,800
then the data should
continue moving left.

373
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:08,340
And so then, at that point,
the distribution over actions

374
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:12,460
should look something
more like this

375
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:16,220
once you are in that
state at that point.

376
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:18,060
So if you have a good
demonstration data

377
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:20,240
set and a good state
representation,

378
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:22,480
if you perfectly model
the demonstrations,

379
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:25,380
you will get a good policy out.

380
00:19:25,380 --> 00:19:27,517
So the question is, is
the only goal in imitation

381
00:19:27,517 --> 00:19:29,850
learning to mimic the expert
or is there some assumption

382
00:19:29,850 --> 00:19:33,450
that the expert was
maximizing some reward?

383
00:19:33,450 --> 00:19:37,650
Generally, if you assume that
what you're trying to imitate

384
00:19:37,650 --> 00:19:42,210
is good, but it
doesn't necessarily

385
00:19:42,210 --> 00:19:44,490
need to be the optimal policy--

386
00:19:44,490 --> 00:19:49,030
and in many real world
practical, messy scenarios,

387
00:19:49,030 --> 00:19:52,565
getting the optimal policy is
like hopeless in some ways.

388
00:19:52,565 --> 00:19:55,190
Generally we assume that you're
trying to mimic something good,

389
00:19:55,190 --> 00:19:58,650
and really the focus is
on trying to imitate that.

390
00:19:58,650 --> 00:20:02,650
So the question is, in this
case, if we do this perfectly,

391
00:20:02,650 --> 00:20:07,090
are we happy with sampling a
mode that is underrepresented?

392
00:20:07,090 --> 00:20:09,530
There are scenarios
where you might

393
00:20:09,530 --> 00:20:11,870
want to try to essentially
do better than the expert.

394
00:20:11,870 --> 00:20:14,390
And maybe in some cases this
mode is actually better,

395
00:20:14,390 --> 00:20:17,770
or maybe in some cases
this mode is really better.

396
00:20:17,770 --> 00:20:19,990
If you want to
outperform the expert,

397
00:20:19,990 --> 00:20:22,170
that's more where
reinforcement learning comes in

398
00:20:22,170 --> 00:20:23,343
and other tools come in.

399
00:20:23,343 --> 00:20:25,010
You can also try to
curate your data set

400
00:20:25,010 --> 00:20:29,870
to try to contain more of the
stuff that you think is better.

401
00:20:29,870 --> 00:20:32,253
But yeah, with
imitation learning,

402
00:20:32,253 --> 00:20:34,670
generally everything that we'll
talk about in this lecture

403
00:20:34,670 --> 00:20:36,710
is trying to meet
expert performance

404
00:20:36,710 --> 00:20:39,802
or meet the demonstrator
performance and not exceed it.

405
00:20:39,802 --> 00:20:41,510
So the question is,
should the parameters

406
00:20:41,510 --> 00:20:44,090
of the distribution over
actions be state dependent?

407
00:20:44,090 --> 00:20:45,470
And yeah, absolutely.

408
00:20:45,470 --> 00:20:48,590
I've been writing this as
the parameters of p of a.

409
00:20:48,590 --> 00:20:50,012
But everything
here is implicitly

410
00:20:50,012 --> 00:20:51,470
dependent on the
state because this

411
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:54,670
is the output of a
neural network that

412
00:20:54,670 --> 00:20:56,990
was conditioned on the state.

413
00:20:56,990 --> 00:20:59,910
And so in some states, it might
choose to output a distribution

414
00:20:59,910 --> 00:21:01,210
that's really multimodal.

415
00:21:01,210 --> 00:21:03,750
In other cases, it might
choose to output a distribution

416
00:21:03,750 --> 00:21:07,490
that is much more unimodal.

417
00:21:07,490 --> 00:21:09,870
So let's talk about
some more interesting

418
00:21:09,870 --> 00:21:12,350
expressive distributions
with neural networks.

419
00:21:12,350 --> 00:21:13,990
And some of these
will build upon tools

420
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:15,950
in generative modeling.

421
00:21:15,950 --> 00:21:19,790
And in particular, if we
think about some examples

422
00:21:19,790 --> 00:21:21,470
of generative
models, we won't go

423
00:21:21,470 --> 00:21:23,090
into all the details of these.

424
00:21:23,090 --> 00:21:25,420
But one example of
a generative model

425
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:28,620
that you might have seen before
is called a diffusion model,

426
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:32,380
and this is a model that
can output and generate

427
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:35,280
images of different people,
can be used to generate videos,

428
00:21:35,280 --> 00:21:38,060
and so forth, is very
popular for generative models

429
00:21:38,060 --> 00:21:40,460
over visual data.

430
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:44,540
And the thing that's really
cool about these image diffusion

431
00:21:44,540 --> 00:21:47,340
models is that they
are able to represent

432
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:49,900
a very diverse distribution
over images, including

433
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:55,340
images that are completely
different from one another.

434
00:21:55,340 --> 00:21:57,020
Another example of
a generative model

435
00:21:57,020 --> 00:21:59,020
is an autoregressive model.

436
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:01,780
You can think about
this as essentially what

437
00:22:01,780 --> 00:22:05,900
happens with language modeling,
where you're predicting one

438
00:22:05,900 --> 00:22:09,460
token or one word at a time.

439
00:22:09,460 --> 00:22:11,520
And this is also a
really powerful model.

440
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:17,243
And it's much more powerful
than just outputting

441
00:22:17,243 --> 00:22:19,660
the distribution, like the
mean and variance of a Gaussian

442
00:22:19,660 --> 00:22:21,860
and much more powerful
than outputting,

443
00:22:21,860 --> 00:22:26,440
for example, the categorical
distribution over one dimension.

444
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:30,640
So you can think about these
two as most typically used

445
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:33,760
to represent either the
probability of an image

446
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:37,680
given a text description or the
probability of the next word

447
00:22:37,680 --> 00:22:40,240
given the word so far.

448
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:43,440
And the reason why they're
often used in these contexts

449
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:47,360
is because these are
very common problems

450
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:49,080
that people work
on a lot, and also

451
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:53,040
because they're very well suited
for addressing these problems.

452
00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:57,280
But the underlying technique
behind image diffusion models

453
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:00,040
and autoregressive models
is extremely general,

454
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:02,720
and you can use the
same idea to learn

455
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:05,200
a distribution
over actions given

456
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:08,820
observations or given state.

457
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:10,320
So let's talk a
little bit about how

458
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:14,020
we might use some of these tools
to learn that distribution.

459
00:23:14,020 --> 00:23:16,720

460
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:21,580
So our goal is to
approximate p of a given s.

461
00:23:21,580 --> 00:23:25,415
One maybe form of
generative model

462
00:23:25,415 --> 00:23:27,540
that's simpler than those
two that we'll start with

463
00:23:27,540 --> 00:23:29,460
is a mixture of Gaussians.

464
00:23:29,460 --> 00:23:32,180
And a mixture of Gaussians is
already a lot more expressive

465
00:23:32,180 --> 00:23:34,380
than just a single
Gaussian, and so it's

466
00:23:34,380 --> 00:23:36,820
a case where we're trying
to model a distribution

467
00:23:36,820 --> 00:23:39,380
as a combination of Gaussians.

468
00:23:39,380 --> 00:23:43,900
And so in this case, instead of
just having the neural network

469
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:49,420
output mu, we'll have it output
the parameters of a Gaussian

470
00:23:49,420 --> 00:23:51,120
mixture model distribution.

471
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:57,900
And so in the case
of a GMM, it will

472
00:23:57,900 --> 00:24:04,900
output the mean standard
deviation and weight associated

473
00:24:04,900 --> 00:24:10,140
with one Gaussian, also the mean
standard deviation and weight

474
00:24:10,140 --> 00:24:13,020
of a Gaussian, and so on.

475
00:24:13,020 --> 00:24:15,540
And if you have n
different components,

476
00:24:15,540 --> 00:24:19,530
it will output n
different parameters

477
00:24:19,530 --> 00:24:22,810
for different Gaussians.

478
00:24:22,810 --> 00:24:26,750
So the question is, can
we elaborate more on,

479
00:24:26,750 --> 00:24:29,930
what does it mean for
these distributions

480
00:24:29,930 --> 00:24:32,130
to be more or less expressive?

481
00:24:32,130 --> 00:24:38,730
So essentially, if you have
some underlying distribution,

482
00:24:38,730 --> 00:24:41,250
like how--

483
00:24:41,250 --> 00:24:41,750
let's see.

484
00:24:41,750 --> 00:24:43,958
Maybe I'll actually rephrase
it slightly differently.

485
00:24:43,958 --> 00:24:46,050
So one way to think
about it is it's

486
00:24:46,050 --> 00:24:48,330
essentially the number of
different distributions

487
00:24:48,330 --> 00:24:55,010
you can capture perfectly with
that chosen representation.

488
00:24:55,010 --> 00:24:58,330
And so if you only
use a Gaussian,

489
00:24:58,330 --> 00:25:01,210
then you can only perfectly
model Gaussian distributions.

490
00:25:01,210 --> 00:25:03,530
Whereas if you use
Gaussian mixture model,

491
00:25:03,530 --> 00:25:05,550
you can represent any
individual Gaussian,

492
00:25:05,550 --> 00:25:08,810
but you can also
represent any distribution

493
00:25:08,810 --> 00:25:10,330
that can be
represented perfectly

494
00:25:10,330 --> 00:25:11,790
as a combination of Gaussians.

495
00:25:11,790 --> 00:25:14,330
And so it's strictly more
expressive than using

496
00:25:14,330 --> 00:25:15,770
a single Gaussian.

497
00:25:15,770 --> 00:25:19,818
Now in some cases, it can
be difficult to compare

498
00:25:19,818 --> 00:25:22,110
two different distribution
classes because in some ways

499
00:25:22,110 --> 00:25:24,250
some might be more expressive
or less expressive.

500
00:25:24,250 --> 00:25:26,050
But in the case of
Gaussian mixture models,

501
00:25:26,050 --> 00:25:29,850
it's strictly more
expressive than a Gaussian.

502
00:25:29,850 --> 00:25:32,350
The question is, do you need
to pick the number of Gaussians

503
00:25:32,350 --> 00:25:33,310
ahead of time?

504
00:25:33,310 --> 00:25:34,178
Yes, you do.

505
00:25:34,178 --> 00:25:35,970
You can think about it
as a hyperparameter,

506
00:25:35,970 --> 00:25:38,512
and you could experiment and
look at how well are you fitting

507
00:25:38,512 --> 00:25:41,770
the data with different
numbers of mixture components.

508
00:25:41,770 --> 00:25:45,490

509
00:25:45,490 --> 00:25:48,350
And then there's two other
examples of generative models

510
00:25:48,350 --> 00:25:50,510
that we could use.

511
00:25:50,510 --> 00:25:54,790
The second one is discretized
and autoregressive,

512
00:25:54,790 --> 00:25:56,570
which is just like
language models.

513
00:25:56,570 --> 00:26:00,510
And the third would be to
use something like diffusion

514
00:26:00,510 --> 00:26:05,003
over actions instead of
diffusion over images.

515
00:26:05,003 --> 00:26:06,670
And so we talked a
little bit, just now,

516
00:26:06,670 --> 00:26:08,310
about mixtures of
Gaussians where

517
00:26:08,310 --> 00:26:12,030
we'll be outputting basically
the parameters of a Gaussian

518
00:26:12,030 --> 00:26:15,030
mixture model.

519
00:26:15,030 --> 00:26:19,620
Now let's talk a little
bit about a scenario where

520
00:26:19,620 --> 00:26:22,900
we could try to use an
autoregressive model

521
00:26:22,900 --> 00:26:28,620
like a language model in
order to generate action.

522
00:26:28,620 --> 00:26:33,140
So in this case, there is
a couple scenarios where

523
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:35,220
you might want to use this.

524
00:26:35,220 --> 00:26:38,180
So you might be in a scenario
where your actions are already

525
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:40,100
discrete.

526
00:26:40,100 --> 00:26:43,460
And you actually don't need
to discretize in that case.

527
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:46,692
Or you might be in a scenario
where it's multidimensional,

528
00:26:46,692 --> 00:26:48,900
or you might be in a scenario
where they are actually

529
00:26:48,900 --> 00:26:51,000
continuous and
multi-dimensional,

530
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:54,300
and then you need to discretize
or tokenize first, and then

531
00:26:54,300 --> 00:26:56,140
use an autoregressive model.

532
00:26:56,140 --> 00:27:03,100
And so if we think about this
back in the driving example,

533
00:27:03,100 --> 00:27:11,500
if we use discretize
plus autoregressive--

534
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:14,220

535
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:16,140
let's say that we're
in the driving case,

536
00:27:16,140 --> 00:27:20,960
and we'll just consider
a simplified driving case

537
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:27,240
where the first action
is the steering angle,

538
00:27:27,240 --> 00:27:31,060
and the second action
is the acceleration.

539
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:34,160

540
00:27:34,160 --> 00:27:39,200
So if we wanted to represent a
distribution over these actions

541
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:42,480
using an autoregressive
model, there's two things

542
00:27:42,480 --> 00:27:45,120
that we would want to do.

543
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:46,500
The first would
be to discretize,

544
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:48,360
and the second then
would be to formulate

545
00:27:48,360 --> 00:27:49,760
the autoregressive model.

546
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:52,510
And in particular, when we
use autoregressive models,

547
00:27:52,510 --> 00:27:54,760
it means that we're going
to be essentially predicting

548
00:27:54,760 --> 00:27:57,360
one dimension at a time.

549
00:27:57,360 --> 00:27:59,320
So we first want to
model a distribution

550
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:02,320
over the first
dimension of the action.

551
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:05,860
What we would do is, in
the case of steering--

552
00:28:05,860 --> 00:28:10,830
so we have P steering.

553
00:28:10,830 --> 00:28:13,670

554
00:28:13,670 --> 00:28:16,710
Say that we wanted
our prediction

555
00:28:16,710 --> 00:28:19,630
to look something like this.

556
00:28:19,630 --> 00:28:21,490
Now, a Gaussian
can't represent this.

557
00:28:21,490 --> 00:28:24,870
And so we would choose to
discretize it and represent

558
00:28:24,870 --> 00:28:26,530
a discretized distribution.

559
00:28:26,530 --> 00:28:29,070
And what that would
look like is essentially

560
00:28:29,070 --> 00:28:34,990
creating bins for the
different steering angles,

561
00:28:34,990 --> 00:28:41,350
and you would then output
a probability for each bin.

562
00:28:41,350 --> 00:28:46,750
And then something that
looks a little bit closer

563
00:28:46,750 --> 00:28:52,230
to a staircase, these bars would
be the distribution that you're

564
00:28:52,230 --> 00:28:56,010
actually using to approximate
the original distribution.

565
00:28:56,010 --> 00:29:03,290

566
00:29:03,290 --> 00:29:04,990
So for the first
dimension, you would

567
00:29:04,990 --> 00:29:07,270
be outputting each
of the probability

568
00:29:07,270 --> 00:29:09,670
values for each of these
bars, like the height

569
00:29:09,670 --> 00:29:12,010
of each of these bars.

570
00:29:12,010 --> 00:29:14,730
So this gives you a fairly
expressive distribution

571
00:29:14,730 --> 00:29:15,555
over angle.

572
00:29:15,555 --> 00:29:16,930
And then you could
choose to have

573
00:29:16,930 --> 00:29:20,350
a more fine discretization or
a more coarse discretization.

574
00:29:20,350 --> 00:29:23,770
And if it's more fine, you
get more expressivity in terms

575
00:29:23,770 --> 00:29:24,922
of what you can represent.

576
00:29:24,922 --> 00:29:26,630
But you also have to
output more numbers,

577
00:29:26,630 --> 00:29:28,690
so there's a trade-off there.

578
00:29:28,690 --> 00:29:32,850
And then once you output a
distribution over the angle,

579
00:29:32,850 --> 00:29:37,090
then you would want to output a
distribution over acceleration.

580
00:29:37,090 --> 00:29:40,050
And in particular, in
the case of merging

581
00:29:40,050 --> 00:29:42,322
left versus going straight,
if you're merging left,

582
00:29:42,322 --> 00:29:44,530
probably you would be
accelerating more because there

583
00:29:44,530 --> 00:29:46,455
was a car back into the left.

584
00:29:46,455 --> 00:29:47,830
Whereas if you're
going straight,

585
00:29:47,830 --> 00:29:53,970
you're probably more likely to
be keeping your current speed.

586
00:29:53,970 --> 00:30:00,170
And so we want our
distribution over acceleration

587
00:30:00,170 --> 00:30:04,320
to be conditioned on which
steering angle we chose.

588
00:30:04,320 --> 00:30:06,930

589
00:30:06,930 --> 00:30:13,990
So what we'll do is
we'll predict p angle,

590
00:30:13,990 --> 00:30:18,730
and then we will sample a
steering angle from this.

591
00:30:18,730 --> 00:30:22,070
We'll refer to
this for now as at1

592
00:30:22,070 --> 00:30:25,210
because it's the first
dimension of the action.

593
00:30:25,210 --> 00:30:29,030
We'll sample it from
this distribution here.

594
00:30:29,030 --> 00:30:32,750
And then we want to
get a distribution

595
00:30:32,750 --> 00:30:36,590
over the second
dimension conditioned

596
00:30:36,590 --> 00:30:39,550
on the first dimension.

597
00:30:39,550 --> 00:30:42,350
And to do this, we'll then
pass our predicted action

598
00:30:42,350 --> 00:30:45,070
or the sampled action
back into our model

599
00:30:45,070 --> 00:30:48,470
and then have our model
output another distribution.

600
00:30:48,470 --> 00:30:57,750
And in this case, if you
are conditioning on at1

601
00:30:57,750 --> 00:31:02,910
equals maybe an angle that's
negative 2, so something more

602
00:31:02,910 --> 00:31:07,620
left, then you might output a
distribution over acceleration

603
00:31:07,620 --> 00:31:16,500
that has more probability
on higher accelerations,

604
00:31:16,500 --> 00:31:23,460
versus if you condition on a
steering angle that is something

605
00:31:23,460 --> 00:31:27,300
more like 0, then you
might output a distribution

606
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:33,500
over accelerations
that is smaller.

607
00:31:33,500 --> 00:31:36,300
So the question is, how do you
build your training data when

608
00:31:36,300 --> 00:31:38,730
you are discretizing like this?

609
00:31:38,730 --> 00:31:44,500

610
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:45,040
Let's see.

611
00:31:45,040 --> 00:31:47,660
So when you build
your training data,

612
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:51,140
you'll have the
state action pairs,

613
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:53,740
and you'll be sampling
state action pairs.

614
00:31:53,740 --> 00:31:59,333
And in order to convert it into
something like this, what you'll

615
00:31:59,333 --> 00:32:01,000
do is you'll sample
a state action pair,

616
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:03,700
then you'll bin the
action according

617
00:32:03,700 --> 00:32:05,800
to your bidding strategy.

618
00:32:05,800 --> 00:32:10,640
And then you'll
apply a loss that

619
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:13,600
is between the model's
predicted distribution

620
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:17,347
and the sampled action, which
would be like one of these bins.

621
00:32:17,347 --> 00:32:19,680
And the loss you can actually
just use the cross entropy

622
00:32:19,680 --> 00:32:20,760
loss here.

623
00:32:20,760 --> 00:32:22,440
And so you can think
about it as you're

624
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:25,720
doing a classification
problem for each dimension

625
00:32:25,720 --> 00:32:29,080
of your actions.

626
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:31,440
One other diagram that I
think would be helpful here

627
00:32:31,440 --> 00:32:38,240
to think about how we go about
doing this is when you have

628
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:41,720
a model that's autoregressive,
that's predicting actions,

629
00:32:41,720 --> 00:32:44,040
you often use a sequence model.

630
00:32:44,040 --> 00:32:47,612
And so if you think about
a sequence neural network,

631
00:32:47,612 --> 00:32:49,320
something like a
transformer or something

632
00:32:49,320 --> 00:32:53,600
like that, you would
pass as input your state.

633
00:32:53,600 --> 00:33:00,000
And then you're
predicting a distribution

634
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:05,470
over the first dimension
of your action.

635
00:33:05,470 --> 00:33:08,590
So this is a distribution
over steering angle.

636
00:33:08,590 --> 00:33:14,110
You would then sample
from this distribution,

637
00:33:14,110 --> 00:33:20,990
and then you'll pass this
sampled action or your chosen

638
00:33:20,990 --> 00:33:24,470
steering command back
into your model, and then

639
00:33:24,470 --> 00:33:28,890
output the distribution
over the next action.

640
00:33:28,890 --> 00:33:30,810
This is exactly how
language models work,

641
00:33:30,810 --> 00:33:35,650
where you'll have
some beginning token.

642
00:33:35,650 --> 00:33:37,890
You'll predict a distribution
over the next word.

643
00:33:37,890 --> 00:33:41,550
You'll sample a word from that,
pass that sampled word back

644
00:33:41,550 --> 00:33:44,510
into the model, and then
predict a distribution

645
00:33:44,510 --> 00:33:46,870
over the next word,
except now we're

646
00:33:46,870 --> 00:33:49,430
doing it over dimensions
of our action sequence

647
00:33:49,430 --> 00:33:54,105
rather than over different
words in a sentence.

648
00:33:54,105 --> 00:33:54,730
Great question.

649
00:33:54,730 --> 00:33:57,750
How do you determine the order
of these different dimensions?

650
00:33:57,750 --> 00:33:59,310
In language models,
it's very natural

651
00:33:59,310 --> 00:34:02,900
because there's a natural
ordering to words in a sentence,

652
00:34:02,900 --> 00:34:05,150
for example, although even
across different languages,

653
00:34:05,150 --> 00:34:07,370
there's some variation there.

654
00:34:07,370 --> 00:34:10,870
It's something that
generally you pick by hand.

655
00:34:10,870 --> 00:34:13,630
You could also, in some ways
treat it as a hyperparameter.

656
00:34:13,630 --> 00:34:16,130
In other cases, there might
also be somewhat natural choices

657
00:34:16,130 --> 00:34:17,230
for the ordering.

658
00:34:17,230 --> 00:34:18,790
So if you're
controlling a robot arm,

659
00:34:18,790 --> 00:34:21,130
for example, you
can go down what's

660
00:34:21,130 --> 00:34:23,310
called the kinematic
chain of a robot arm

661
00:34:23,310 --> 00:34:25,670
where you start with
the base dimension,

662
00:34:25,670 --> 00:34:28,170
and then you go to the next
joint and the next joint,

663
00:34:28,170 --> 00:34:30,489
and so forth.

664
00:34:30,489 --> 00:34:34,449
The good news is
that, in principle,

665
00:34:34,449 --> 00:34:37,409
the expressive power
of this distribution

666
00:34:37,409 --> 00:34:39,330
doesn't change with the order.

667
00:34:39,330 --> 00:34:41,449
Essentially, your goal
is trying to represent

668
00:34:41,449 --> 00:34:44,290
the full joint distribution
over all dimensions.

669
00:34:44,290 --> 00:34:47,449
And the only reason why
you're doing an ordering here

670
00:34:47,449 --> 00:34:52,889
is because, if you tried to
represent a joint distribution

671
00:34:52,889 --> 00:34:54,570
over all the dimensions
at once, there's

672
00:34:54,570 --> 00:34:58,170
a lot of pairwise probabilities
that you would have to predict,

673
00:34:58,170 --> 00:35:01,400
and it's a lot more efficient
to do it one dimension at a time

674
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:04,240
to represent the full joint.

675
00:35:04,240 --> 00:35:07,142
So the question is, in
the case of a video game,

676
00:35:07,142 --> 00:35:08,100
it was pretty discrete.

677
00:35:08,100 --> 00:35:11,500
Here we're in a more
continuous case.

678
00:35:11,500 --> 00:35:13,500
Whenever we want to use
an autoregressive model,

679
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:15,220
does that mean we
should be discretizing?

680
00:35:15,220 --> 00:35:16,100
Right?

681
00:35:16,100 --> 00:35:17,280
Cool.

682
00:35:17,280 --> 00:35:24,720
So there's actually two reasons
to be using discretization.

683
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:26,280
One reason to use
discretization is

684
00:35:26,280 --> 00:35:30,560
it allows you to model a very
multimodal complex distribution.

685
00:35:30,560 --> 00:35:34,102
And so these bars can be
any height that you want.

686
00:35:34,102 --> 00:35:35,560
And so it actually
can model things

687
00:35:35,560 --> 00:35:38,280
that are way more expressive
than a mixture of Gaussians,

688
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:42,745
up to how fine your
discretization is.

689
00:35:42,745 --> 00:35:44,120
The challenge with
discretization

690
00:35:44,120 --> 00:35:45,860
is that if you have
a lot of bins--

691
00:35:45,860 --> 00:35:48,100
you might have hundreds
bins for one dimension,

692
00:35:48,100 --> 00:35:50,680
and then you're trying to
represent, I don't know,

693
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:53,000
10 dimensional action
or something like that,

694
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:56,640
then the dimensionality of
your discretized actions

695
00:35:56,640 --> 00:35:59,540
is going to blow up
if you try to model

696
00:35:59,540 --> 00:36:03,400
that full 10 to the fifth
matrix over probabilities.

697
00:36:03,400 --> 00:36:05,220
And so using an
autoregressive model

698
00:36:05,220 --> 00:36:09,020
helps you deal with
that dimensionality.

699
00:36:09,020 --> 00:36:12,260
You could, in principle, also
use an autoregressive model

700
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:15,740
with a mixture of
Gaussians, for example,

701
00:36:15,740 --> 00:36:18,560
and that would be
completely fine as well.

702
00:36:18,560 --> 00:36:21,680

703
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:24,340
It's not like a
common choice per se,

704
00:36:24,340 --> 00:36:26,980
because if you're going to go
through the trouble of using

705
00:36:26,980 --> 00:36:29,100
an autoregressive model,
usually discretization

706
00:36:29,100 --> 00:36:32,500
is a pretty powerful choice.

707
00:36:32,500 --> 00:36:35,100
But yeah, you can also use
it with a Gaussian mixture

708
00:36:35,100 --> 00:36:37,820
model or Gaussians or
something like that.

709
00:36:37,820 --> 00:36:40,800
So the question was around
how we actually train this.

710
00:36:40,800 --> 00:36:42,660
And maybe let's just
walk through how

711
00:36:42,660 --> 00:36:44,000
we go about training this.

712
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:46,640
So it's actually going to look--

713
00:36:46,640 --> 00:36:49,382

714
00:36:49,382 --> 00:36:50,840
Let's walk through
how we train it.

715
00:36:50,840 --> 00:36:52,620
So we're given some data set.

716
00:36:52,620 --> 00:36:58,810
So we're given some data set
that has state action pairs,

717
00:36:58,810 --> 00:37:02,070
and we're going to want to
train our neural network.

718
00:37:02,070 --> 00:37:04,370
And we're going to
follow a typical way

719
00:37:04,370 --> 00:37:05,890
of training neural
networks which

720
00:37:05,890 --> 00:37:11,930
is to sample an example
from your data set,

721
00:37:11,930 --> 00:37:14,090
or sample a mini
batch of examples.

722
00:37:14,090 --> 00:37:16,930
I'll write it up for a
mini batch size of 1, just

723
00:37:16,930 --> 00:37:20,210
for simplicity, but
you can extend it.

724
00:37:20,210 --> 00:37:23,610
So we're going to be
sampling a state action

725
00:37:23,610 --> 00:37:26,130
pair from our data set.

726
00:37:26,130 --> 00:37:37,575
And once we sample this,
we will essentially

727
00:37:37,575 --> 00:37:39,450
be running a forward
pass through our network

728
00:37:39,450 --> 00:37:41,290
and a backward pass
through our network.

729
00:37:41,290 --> 00:37:43,210
And the key question
that comes up

730
00:37:43,210 --> 00:37:48,010
is doing the forward pass
is fairly straightforward.

731
00:37:48,010 --> 00:37:49,650
And when we do the
forward pass, we'll

732
00:37:49,650 --> 00:37:53,890
be getting some probability over
the actions of each dimension.

733
00:37:53,890 --> 00:37:59,070
And then we need to figure out
what should the objective be.

734
00:37:59,070 --> 00:38:01,510
There's actually
a common objective

735
00:38:01,510 --> 00:38:05,590
that we can think about for
all of these generative models.

736
00:38:05,590 --> 00:38:10,470
And that objective is that we're
outputting these probability

737
00:38:10,470 --> 00:38:11,510
distributions.

738
00:38:11,510 --> 00:38:14,510
We want to be maximizing the
probability of the actions

739
00:38:14,510 --> 00:38:16,710
represented in the data.

740
00:38:16,710 --> 00:38:20,590
Or equivalently,
we'll write this

741
00:38:20,590 --> 00:38:28,230
as minimizing the
log probability of--

742
00:38:28,230 --> 00:38:34,470
I'll write this as pi
theta of a given S,

743
00:38:34,470 --> 00:38:37,368
and this is with
respect to theta.

744
00:38:37,368 --> 00:38:38,910
And so what this
means is we're going

745
00:38:38,910 --> 00:38:41,870
to be evaluating how
likely is the action that

746
00:38:41,870 --> 00:38:43,880
was sampled under our policy.

747
00:38:43,880 --> 00:38:46,430

748
00:38:46,430 --> 00:38:51,030
And then we'll be trying to make
the probability of the action

749
00:38:51,030 --> 00:38:54,290
in the data set as high as
possible, or the negative log

750
00:38:54,290 --> 00:38:56,970
probability as low as possible.

751
00:38:56,970 --> 00:38:59,450
Now, in practice, what
this log probability

752
00:38:59,450 --> 00:39:03,290
looks like is going to be
different for different models.

753
00:39:03,290 --> 00:39:05,330
For a Gaussian mixture
model, what you'll do

754
00:39:05,330 --> 00:39:07,730
is you can write down
the probability density

755
00:39:07,730 --> 00:39:10,970
function for a
Gaussian mixture model,

756
00:39:10,970 --> 00:39:15,850
and then just differentiate with
respect to the mean, variance,

757
00:39:15,850 --> 00:39:19,890
and weight that is outputted
by the neural network.

758
00:39:19,890 --> 00:39:26,143
And for the case of a
autoregressive model,

759
00:39:26,143 --> 00:39:27,310
it's a little bit different.

760
00:39:27,310 --> 00:39:32,690
So what this looks like for
the autoregressive model is

761
00:39:32,690 --> 00:39:44,850
we essentially get
the probability of at1

762
00:39:44,850 --> 00:39:52,320
and the probability of at2
given at1 and so forth.

763
00:39:52,320 --> 00:39:58,020
And this probability,
for this first dimension,

764
00:39:58,020 --> 00:40:00,920
it will just be the
cross-entropy loss

765
00:40:00,920 --> 00:40:04,240
that corresponds to
trying to maximize the log

766
00:40:04,240 --> 00:40:11,320
probability of this value for a
discrete categorical variable.

767
00:40:11,320 --> 00:40:14,103
And for the second
dimension, that's

768
00:40:14,103 --> 00:40:15,520
where things get
a little bit more

769
00:40:15,520 --> 00:40:19,660
complicated because this will
just be the cross-entropy loss.

770
00:40:19,660 --> 00:40:24,120
But you need to figure out which
action you want to use here,

771
00:40:24,120 --> 00:40:28,840
and oftentimes you will use
the action in the data set.

772
00:40:28,840 --> 00:40:31,720
So for the inputs,
you'll often actually

773
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:35,000
pass as input the state
and the dimensions that

774
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:36,340
were chosen in the data set.

775
00:40:36,340 --> 00:40:38,520
And then for each of
these probabilities,

776
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:41,122
you'll be applying
the cross-entropy loss

777
00:40:41,122 --> 00:40:43,080
and backpropagating the
cross-entropy loss back

778
00:40:43,080 --> 00:40:44,800
into the network.

779
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:47,320
And then at test time,
you'll then actually

780
00:40:47,320 --> 00:40:51,020
be using your own predictions
and passing your own predictions

781
00:40:51,020 --> 00:40:53,980
into the neural network.

782
00:40:53,980 --> 00:40:59,580
But this objective right here is
really the core loss function.

783
00:40:59,580 --> 00:41:04,020
And this corresponds to a
form of cross-entropy loss

784
00:41:04,020 --> 00:41:06,380
for discretized distributions.

785
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:08,060
And for Gaussian
mixture models, you

786
00:41:08,060 --> 00:41:10,900
write down the PDF
and differentiate.

787
00:41:10,900 --> 00:41:14,420
So the question was, it seems a
little odd to discretize and do

788
00:41:14,420 --> 00:41:17,287
a categorical distribution
over continuous variable,

789
00:41:17,287 --> 00:41:19,620
especially given that a loss
function like cross-entropy

790
00:41:19,620 --> 00:41:24,500
won't take into account the fact
that two bins error is probably

791
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:29,060
less costly than an error for
two bins that are very far

792
00:41:29,060 --> 00:41:31,300
apart from one another.

793
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:38,820
This is one downside to
using discretized actions.

794
00:41:38,820 --> 00:41:41,780
In practice, often that
distance is something

795
00:41:41,780 --> 00:41:45,980
that is fairly quick to learn.

796
00:41:45,980 --> 00:41:51,050
And so the outcome is
often still pretty good,

797
00:41:51,050 --> 00:41:54,230
but you might need more data.

798
00:41:54,230 --> 00:41:57,410
And how fine your discretization
is a really important

799
00:41:57,410 --> 00:41:59,730
hyperparameter because
if it's too fine,

800
00:41:59,730 --> 00:42:01,290
then it'll be very
hard to get signal

801
00:42:01,290 --> 00:42:05,270
because if you're off by 1,
you'll get 0 loss function.

802
00:42:05,270 --> 00:42:06,770
There are also other
things that you

803
00:42:06,770 --> 00:42:10,410
can do to try to
smoothen out the data

804
00:42:10,410 --> 00:42:15,330
and smoothen out the labels
so that the label isn't just

805
00:42:15,330 --> 00:42:16,550
like a one hot vector.

806
00:42:16,550 --> 00:42:18,955
You can smooth around
it a little bit.

807
00:42:18,955 --> 00:42:20,330
So that's one
thing that can also

808
00:42:20,330 --> 00:42:24,130
help with that, although it's
a little bit more advanced.

809
00:42:24,130 --> 00:42:24,710
Oh, yeah.

810
00:42:24,710 --> 00:42:25,418
Awesome question.

811
00:42:25,418 --> 00:42:28,380
I didn't actually get to
write the third example.

812
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:31,030

813
00:42:31,030 --> 00:42:35,390
So when you are outputting the
probability of the third action,

814
00:42:35,390 --> 00:42:37,810
it will be conditioned
on everything so far.

815
00:42:37,810 --> 00:42:40,090
And often the way
that you do that,

816
00:42:40,090 --> 00:42:41,970
is if you're using
a sequence model,

817
00:42:41,970 --> 00:42:45,370
you can then output the
prediction like a sample

818
00:42:45,370 --> 00:42:52,270
from this distribution, so at2,
and then output the probability

819
00:42:52,270 --> 00:42:54,010
of the third action right here.

820
00:42:54,010 --> 00:42:55,990
And if you have a
sequence model that

821
00:42:55,990 --> 00:42:58,130
takes into account
everything from the past,

822
00:42:58,130 --> 00:43:01,710
then it will already condition
on all the actions so far.

823
00:43:01,710 --> 00:43:06,010
So how does backpropagation
handle this when it's discrete?

824
00:43:06,010 --> 00:43:09,870
So only the output is discrete.

825
00:43:09,870 --> 00:43:12,430
And so you can think about it
as a classification problem.

826
00:43:12,430 --> 00:43:14,070
And so if the
weights are discrete,

827
00:43:14,070 --> 00:43:17,530
that's bad for backpropagation.

828
00:43:17,530 --> 00:43:19,190
But when it's only
at the labels,

829
00:43:19,190 --> 00:43:21,190
you're only just computing
the loss function

830
00:43:21,190 --> 00:43:25,710
on those discrete variables,
and then that loss

831
00:43:25,710 --> 00:43:29,910
you can back propagate
in a direct way.

832
00:43:29,910 --> 00:43:33,950
And that's because it's
outputting a probability

833
00:43:33,950 --> 00:43:37,210
and computing a loss
on that probability,

834
00:43:37,210 --> 00:43:40,030
and then you can back propagate
that loss on that probability.

835
00:43:40,030 --> 00:43:42,070
So that outputs
are actually still

836
00:43:42,070 --> 00:43:45,060
continuous values because you're
outputting the height of each

837
00:43:45,060 --> 00:43:45,840
of these bars.

838
00:43:45,840 --> 00:43:49,660

839
00:43:49,660 --> 00:43:50,660
Cool.

840
00:43:50,660 --> 00:43:54,060
So this is a visualization
of this discretized case

841
00:43:54,060 --> 00:43:57,920
where outputting the first
dimension, the second dimension

842
00:43:57,920 --> 00:44:00,300
given the first dimension,
the third dimension given

843
00:44:00,300 --> 00:44:03,620
the first two dimensions of
the action, and so forth.

844
00:44:03,620 --> 00:44:05,200
And this is very scalable.

845
00:44:05,200 --> 00:44:09,660
It allows you to handle fairly
long sequences of actions

846
00:44:09,660 --> 00:44:12,740
similar to language models.

847
00:44:12,740 --> 00:44:15,160
The last thing that I put
up on here is diffusion.

848
00:44:15,160 --> 00:44:18,940
Diffusion is also a very
appealing choice, especially

849
00:44:18,940 --> 00:44:21,140
for continuous actions.

850
00:44:21,140 --> 00:44:23,180
It can model very
complex distributions

851
00:44:23,180 --> 00:44:24,958
over continuous
variables, including

852
00:44:24,958 --> 00:44:26,500
high-dimensional
continuous variables

853
00:44:26,500 --> 00:44:28,020
like images or actions.

854
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:31,020
And it does so by using
this denoising process

855
00:44:31,020 --> 00:44:32,620
where you start
with complete noise,

856
00:44:32,620 --> 00:44:37,500
and denoise it iteratively to
make it a less and less noisy

857
00:44:37,500 --> 00:44:41,060
version of the action to
ultimately generate, hopefully,

858
00:44:41,060 --> 00:44:44,040
a clean action prediction.

859
00:44:44,040 --> 00:44:48,560
Oh, and one thing that's
notable is just both this model

860
00:44:48,560 --> 00:44:52,280
and diffusion models do
have some iterative process.

861
00:44:52,280 --> 00:44:53,980
In the case of
autoregressive models,

862
00:44:53,980 --> 00:44:57,580
it's iterative over the
dimensions of your prediction.

863
00:44:57,580 --> 00:44:59,120
And in the case
of diffusion, it's

864
00:44:59,120 --> 00:45:02,760
iterative over this
denoising process.

865
00:45:02,760 --> 00:45:04,780
Now, one question I
have for all of you.

866
00:45:04,780 --> 00:45:10,840
So we've talked about these more
expressive distribution classes,

867
00:45:10,840 --> 00:45:14,120
and none of what we talked about
has actually really changed

868
00:45:14,120 --> 00:45:16,600
the architecture of the model.

869
00:45:16,600 --> 00:45:20,680
And I'm curious.

870
00:45:20,680 --> 00:45:23,640
One very important
question is, instead

871
00:45:23,640 --> 00:45:27,320
of having these more
complex distributions,

872
00:45:27,320 --> 00:45:31,600
we could just still use the L2
error, the L2 norm as the loss

873
00:45:31,600 --> 00:45:34,987
function, but just make
the neural network bigger.

874
00:45:34,987 --> 00:45:37,320
And bigger neural networks
are generally more expressive

875
00:45:37,320 --> 00:45:39,360
than smaller neural networks.

876
00:45:39,360 --> 00:45:43,980
So does anyone have thoughts on
how what we talked about here

877
00:45:43,980 --> 00:45:47,780
is different from just making
the model bigger with the L2

878
00:45:47,780 --> 00:45:49,340
loss?

879
00:45:49,340 --> 00:45:51,020
So in the driving
example, even if you

880
00:45:51,020 --> 00:45:53,520
make the network
really, really large,

881
00:45:53,520 --> 00:45:55,780
it's still going to
output just the mean.

882
00:45:55,780 --> 00:45:58,220
It might output the mean
very, very accurately,

883
00:45:58,220 --> 00:46:00,540
but it doesn't actually
change its ability

884
00:46:00,540 --> 00:46:03,940
to represent a more
complex distribution.

885
00:46:03,940 --> 00:46:07,780
And so the expressive power
of a distribution that you're

886
00:46:07,780 --> 00:46:11,100
trying to represent is
actually, in many ways,

887
00:46:11,100 --> 00:46:15,700
separate from the expressive
power of the function itself.

888
00:46:15,700 --> 00:46:17,200
And if you think
about this picture,

889
00:46:17,200 --> 00:46:19,075
again, where you have
a neural network that's

890
00:46:19,075 --> 00:46:20,880
outputting parameters
of a distribution,

891
00:46:20,880 --> 00:46:22,740
there's the expressive
part of this function

892
00:46:22,740 --> 00:46:26,100
of how many functions
can you represent

893
00:46:26,100 --> 00:46:28,100
that go from S to parameters.

894
00:46:28,100 --> 00:46:30,683
And then there's the expressive
power of this top part that,

895
00:46:30,683 --> 00:46:33,100
how many different distributions
can those parameters even

896
00:46:33,100 --> 00:46:35,260
represent in the first place?

897
00:46:35,260 --> 00:46:37,340
And this is a really
important point

898
00:46:37,340 --> 00:46:38,980
where neural
network expressivity

899
00:46:38,980 --> 00:46:42,270
is often distinct from
distribution expressivity.

900
00:46:42,270 --> 00:46:50,908

901
00:46:50,908 --> 00:46:52,450
The expressivity of
the distributions

902
00:46:52,450 --> 00:46:55,570
that it can
represent is captured

903
00:46:55,570 --> 00:46:58,855
by what distribution you're
allowing it to represent.

904
00:46:58,855 --> 00:47:00,730
And if it only outputs
the mean and variance,

905
00:47:00,730 --> 00:47:03,250
it can very expressively
represent functions

906
00:47:03,250 --> 00:47:05,070
that go from state
to mean and variance,

907
00:47:05,070 --> 00:47:09,090
but it can't represent anything
more complex than a Gaussian.

908
00:47:09,090 --> 00:47:10,510
So we walk through this a bit.

909
00:47:10,510 --> 00:47:12,190
So we are given
some demonstrations.

910
00:47:12,190 --> 00:47:15,050
We're trying to train a
generative model over actions.

911
00:47:15,050 --> 00:47:21,570
And this is trying to
minimize the negative log

912
00:47:21,570 --> 00:47:23,930
likelihood of the
expert's actions

913
00:47:23,930 --> 00:47:30,450
given the states and
the demonstration.

914
00:47:30,450 --> 00:47:32,610
And so this equation looks,
I mean, maybe a little

915
00:47:32,610 --> 00:47:36,450
bit complicated, but the log
pi is the log probability

916
00:47:36,450 --> 00:47:38,870
under the policy.

917
00:47:38,870 --> 00:47:42,230
The blue part is
the demo actions

918
00:47:42,230 --> 00:47:44,590
that are sampled from the
demonstration data set.

919
00:47:44,590 --> 00:47:47,030
And this probability
is under the policy

920
00:47:47,030 --> 00:47:48,250
that we're trying to learn.

921
00:47:48,250 --> 00:47:52,310

922
00:47:52,310 --> 00:47:54,790
And then once we
fit this, we should

923
00:47:54,790 --> 00:47:58,310
have a policy that's better
at actually matching what

924
00:47:58,310 --> 00:48:00,450
the expert is doing,
and as a result,

925
00:48:00,450 --> 00:48:03,750
it should be better at
performing the task.

926
00:48:03,750 --> 00:48:06,950
So now why do we learn all this?

927
00:48:06,950 --> 00:48:09,630
How well does the
generative model

928
00:48:09,630 --> 00:48:13,390
work compared to just
using an L2 loss?

929
00:48:13,390 --> 00:48:15,750
There's a couple
of works that have

930
00:48:15,750 --> 00:48:17,330
evaluated this more explicitly.

931
00:48:17,330 --> 00:48:19,350
So here's a transport
task where you

932
00:48:19,350 --> 00:48:25,050
need to have one robot transport
a tool to another robot.

933
00:48:25,050 --> 00:48:28,750
And the policy is controlling
both of these robots.

934
00:48:28,750 --> 00:48:32,650
And we'll be comparing diffusion
and a Gaussian mixture model.

935
00:48:32,650 --> 00:48:34,910
Diffusion is a lot
more expressive

936
00:48:34,910 --> 00:48:37,567
than what you can even represent
with a Gaussian mixture model.

937
00:48:37,567 --> 00:48:39,900
And we'll be looking at two
different demonstration data

938
00:48:39,900 --> 00:48:40,400
sets.

939
00:48:40,400 --> 00:48:43,400
One was a data set that was
just collected by one person,

940
00:48:43,400 --> 00:48:44,900
and then the other
one is a data set

941
00:48:44,900 --> 00:48:46,500
that was collected
by multiple people.

942
00:48:46,500 --> 00:48:49,540
And we'll look at success rate.

943
00:48:49,540 --> 00:48:50,840
And we use diffusion.

944
00:48:50,840 --> 00:48:54,240
We see that, on both data sets,
it's able to do pretty well,

945
00:48:54,240 --> 00:48:56,660
although it does degrade with
multiple humans in the data

946
00:48:56,660 --> 00:49:01,140
set, which means that as you add
more diversity in the data set,

947
00:49:01,140 --> 00:49:03,360
performance can go down.

948
00:49:03,360 --> 00:49:04,860
And with the Gaussian
mixture model,

949
00:49:04,860 --> 00:49:08,560
we see that well, first it
does worse than diffusion,

950
00:49:08,560 --> 00:49:12,720
so expressive distributions
is very helpful.

951
00:49:12,720 --> 00:49:15,260
And second it actually
goes down a lot more

952
00:49:15,260 --> 00:49:17,620
for the multi-human
data set, which

953
00:49:17,620 --> 00:49:22,060
means that the expressivity
of the distribution

954
00:49:22,060 --> 00:49:26,340
is especially important for
data sets that have more

955
00:49:26,340 --> 00:49:28,920
different behaviors in them.

956
00:49:28,920 --> 00:49:31,860

957
00:49:31,860 --> 00:49:35,055
We can also see this on a
real task on a real robot.

958
00:49:35,055 --> 00:49:37,180
So this is a task that's
actually pretty difficult,

959
00:49:37,180 --> 00:49:41,240
hanging a shirt on a hanger.

960
00:49:41,240 --> 00:49:44,063
The data set was collected
by lots of different people

961
00:49:44,063 --> 00:49:45,980
because it's actually a
pretty large data set.

962
00:49:45,980 --> 00:49:47,522
And once you scale
up, it's very hard

963
00:49:47,522 --> 00:49:50,000
to have a single person
with one consistent strategy

964
00:49:50,000 --> 00:49:53,360
collect all of your data,
so it has different people

965
00:49:53,360 --> 00:49:55,160
and different strategies.

966
00:49:55,160 --> 00:49:57,200
And it compares using
either diffusion, which

967
00:49:57,200 --> 00:50:00,680
is very expressive,
or an L1 loss, which

968
00:50:00,680 --> 00:50:04,160
is a deterministic policy.

969
00:50:04,160 --> 00:50:07,760
And what we see first, it's not
as successful as the simpler

970
00:50:07,760 --> 00:50:09,080
simulated task.

971
00:50:09,080 --> 00:50:12,360
But here we see again that
having a expressive distribution

972
00:50:12,360 --> 00:50:15,120
is really critical
for being able to do

973
00:50:15,120 --> 00:50:16,100
the task successfully.

974
00:50:16,100 --> 00:50:19,720
And if you just use L1 loss,
the success rate of the policy

975
00:50:19,720 --> 00:50:22,265
drops by nearly 50%.

976
00:50:22,265 --> 00:50:23,640
Is there any
scenario where you'd

977
00:50:23,640 --> 00:50:27,400
want to use a mixture of other
models instead of a Gaussian?

978
00:50:27,400 --> 00:50:33,110
Oftentimes other models might
already be quite expressive.

979
00:50:33,110 --> 00:50:43,390
So not to my knowledge, but
it's completely inconceivable.

980
00:50:43,390 --> 00:50:46,230
Now, if you actually look
at some of the big models

981
00:50:46,230 --> 00:50:50,430
that people have been
developing in robotics,

982
00:50:50,430 --> 00:50:54,910
they build on what we
covered so far in lecture.

983
00:50:54,910 --> 00:50:57,485
And so these are
foundation models trained

984
00:50:57,485 --> 00:50:59,110
on lots of different
demonstration data

985
00:50:59,110 --> 00:51:04,670
sets by companies like NVIDIA,
Figure, Pi, and this open source

986
00:51:04,670 --> 00:51:06,087
vision language model.

987
00:51:06,087 --> 00:51:08,670
Using expressive distributions,
when you have large scale data

988
00:51:08,670 --> 00:51:12,390
sets is a key component
of these models.

989
00:51:12,390 --> 00:51:15,470
And if you look at
some of the big models

990
00:51:15,470 --> 00:51:18,470
that people have been developing
for autonomous driving at Waymo

991
00:51:18,470 --> 00:51:22,030
and Wayve, the first one uses
discretized and autoregressive

992
00:51:22,030 --> 00:51:25,190
prediction, and the second one
also uses discretized actions

993
00:51:25,190 --> 00:51:27,770
with autoregressive prediction.

994
00:51:27,770 --> 00:51:28,270
Great.

995
00:51:28,270 --> 00:51:30,910
So a summary so far of
what we've talked about.

996
00:51:30,910 --> 00:51:33,970
If you have data from one
consistent demonstrator,

997
00:51:33,970 --> 00:51:37,530
you should learn a unimodal
policy distribution.

998
00:51:37,530 --> 00:51:39,910
If you have multimodal data
from multiple demonstrators,

999
00:51:39,910 --> 00:51:41,770
you should learn a distribution.

1000
00:51:41,770 --> 00:51:43,930
You need an expressive
generative model

1001
00:51:43,930 --> 00:51:48,770
when you have data from
multiple people, very important.

1002
00:51:48,770 --> 00:51:51,730
And one other thing
that I'll mention here

1003
00:51:51,730 --> 00:51:54,730
is that the algorithm
is fully offline.

1004
00:51:54,730 --> 00:51:57,090
And this is getting
into a notion that's

1005
00:51:57,090 --> 00:52:00,410
very important in reinforcement
learning more generally,

1006
00:52:00,410 --> 00:52:03,695
which is, offline
algorithms are a bit

1007
00:52:03,695 --> 00:52:05,070
different from
online algorithms.

1008
00:52:05,070 --> 00:52:08,890
So offline algorithms use
only an existing data set,

1009
00:52:08,890 --> 00:52:14,130
and they don't use any new
data from what you're learning.

1010
00:52:14,130 --> 00:52:16,890
And online means that
you're using new data

1011
00:52:16,890 --> 00:52:18,415
from your learned policy.

1012
00:52:18,415 --> 00:52:20,290
And this distinction is
important in a couple

1013
00:52:20,290 --> 00:52:21,690
of different ways.

1014
00:52:21,690 --> 00:52:27,490
Online means that you're going
to have to actually recollect

1015
00:52:27,490 --> 00:52:32,510
data as you run the algorithm,
and so this can be expensive.

1016
00:52:32,510 --> 00:52:36,690
If your policy might be
under-trained, in some scenarios

1017
00:52:36,690 --> 00:52:39,350
it might be unsafe
to run your policy.

1018
00:52:39,350 --> 00:52:41,170
If you're in a medical
domain, for example,

1019
00:52:41,170 --> 00:52:42,712
you might want to
not want to collect

1020
00:52:42,712 --> 00:52:45,340
online data from a policy
that doesn't work fully yet.

1021
00:52:45,340 --> 00:52:48,830

1022
00:52:48,830 --> 00:52:50,270
Thinking about
offline algorithms

1023
00:52:50,270 --> 00:52:54,830
and online algorithms, it's a
key distinction and a key way

1024
00:52:54,830 --> 00:52:58,470
to think about
different algorithms.

1025
00:52:58,470 --> 00:53:01,270
So for what we've
covered so far,

1026
00:53:01,270 --> 00:53:04,510
we don't need any data from
the policy, so this is offline.

1027
00:53:04,510 --> 00:53:07,470
We also don't need to
define a reward function.

1028
00:53:07,470 --> 00:53:10,390
Last time we talked about
defining reward functions

1029
00:53:10,390 --> 00:53:13,670
for some examples, and we talked
about how, in some examples,

1030
00:53:13,670 --> 00:53:16,250
it might be hard to write
down the reward function.

1031
00:53:16,250 --> 00:53:17,430
So this is pretty nice.

1032
00:53:17,430 --> 00:53:19,310
In driving, you don't
have to write down

1033
00:53:19,310 --> 00:53:23,308
all of the ways in which
you might want it to drive.

1034
00:53:23,308 --> 00:53:25,350
The downside is that you
might need a lot of data

1035
00:53:25,350 --> 00:53:26,570
for reliable performance.

1036
00:53:26,570 --> 00:53:29,260
You need a lot of
demonstrations,

1037
00:53:29,260 --> 00:53:33,060
and that ties into how
we might try to learn

1038
00:53:33,060 --> 00:53:36,300
from online interventions.

1039
00:53:36,300 --> 00:53:37,620
So the question was--

1040
00:53:37,620 --> 00:53:40,140
for the previous
videos that I showed--

1041
00:53:40,140 --> 00:53:42,432
imitation learning
seems straightforward,

1042
00:53:42,432 --> 00:53:45,820
what is the research in it?

1043
00:53:45,820 --> 00:53:49,457
And is it just doing what
we talked about today?

1044
00:53:49,457 --> 00:53:51,540
Well, I guess there's a
number of different things

1045
00:53:51,540 --> 00:53:54,430
that went into those
different examples.

1046
00:53:54,430 --> 00:53:57,060

1047
00:53:57,060 --> 00:54:00,000
So I can walk through
maybe a couple examples.

1048
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:04,780
So in the case of the first
demonstration, a lot of things

1049
00:54:04,780 --> 00:54:09,020
went into curation
of the data and also

1050
00:54:09,020 --> 00:54:11,220
the architecture of the model.

1051
00:54:11,220 --> 00:54:14,320
And so those were two things
that were novel about this.

1052
00:54:14,320 --> 00:54:16,660
In the case of the NVIDIA
example, a lot of work

1053
00:54:16,660 --> 00:54:19,580
went into trying to use data
from simulation, especially

1054
00:54:19,580 --> 00:54:22,400
because collecting data on
humanoids is quite challenging,

1055
00:54:22,400 --> 00:54:25,603
and trying to figure out how to
leverage that simulation data.

1056
00:54:25,603 --> 00:54:27,020
In the case of the
figure example,

1057
00:54:27,020 --> 00:54:30,000
there was also some
architectural design choices

1058
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:32,680
that made it helpful, especially
in terms of running models

1059
00:54:32,680 --> 00:54:34,852
at very high frequency.

1060
00:54:34,852 --> 00:54:36,560
And in the OpenVLA
example, that was also

1061
00:54:36,560 --> 00:54:39,240
an example where there was a
lot of architectural choices

1062
00:54:39,240 --> 00:54:42,120
in terms of the models.

1063
00:54:42,120 --> 00:54:44,860
And so it's not just exactly
what we talked about,

1064
00:54:44,860 --> 00:54:46,760
so there is a bit more to it.

1065
00:54:46,760 --> 00:54:49,920
But what we talked about
is a huge driving factor

1066
00:54:49,920 --> 00:54:53,520
behind these different examples.

1067
00:54:53,520 --> 00:54:55,920
And these two, a lot
of that also went

1068
00:54:55,920 --> 00:55:00,200
into different co-training
objectives and architectures.

1069
00:55:00,200 --> 00:55:05,200
So we've talked a lot
about how to train models

1070
00:55:05,200 --> 00:55:06,680
with imitation learning.

1071
00:55:06,680 --> 00:55:09,580
And there's one important thing
that we haven't covered yet,

1072
00:55:09,580 --> 00:55:13,080
which is what can go wrong
and why imitation learning is

1073
00:55:13,080 --> 00:55:14,120
challenging.

1074
00:55:14,120 --> 00:55:17,500
So for this, it's helpful to
look at supervised learning.

1075
00:55:17,500 --> 00:55:21,120
So in supervised learning,
we have some data.

1076
00:55:21,120 --> 00:55:25,020
And it's usually trained from
one data set that stays fixed.

1077
00:55:25,020 --> 00:55:27,990

1078
00:55:27,990 --> 00:55:31,350
And the inputs to the
model are completely

1079
00:55:31,350 --> 00:55:35,310
independent from the predictions
that the model makes.

1080
00:55:35,310 --> 00:55:38,530
Now, this all changes
in imitation learning.

1081
00:55:38,530 --> 00:55:43,810
So say you have a demonstration
that is in your data set.

1082
00:55:43,810 --> 00:55:47,910
And so each of these dots is
like a state and then state

1083
00:55:47,910 --> 00:55:52,710
action, something like this.

1084
00:55:52,710 --> 00:55:57,430
And now you're trying to
mimic this demonstration.

1085
00:55:57,430 --> 00:56:00,070
If you're trying to
mimic it, and then you

1086
00:56:00,070 --> 00:56:03,310
roll out your policy, the
starting from this state,

1087
00:56:03,310 --> 00:56:05,310
maybe at the beginning
of the trajectory,

1088
00:56:05,310 --> 00:56:08,910
it's able to do pretty well.

1089
00:56:08,910 --> 00:56:11,130
But at some point it
might make a mistake.

1090
00:56:11,130 --> 00:56:13,090
The policy isn't
going to be perfect.

1091
00:56:13,090 --> 00:56:15,110
And so if it makes
a mistake, maybe it

1092
00:56:15,110 --> 00:56:16,870
ends up over here
a little further

1093
00:56:16,870 --> 00:56:20,150
from where the
demonstration covered.

1094
00:56:20,150 --> 00:56:21,750
And now from this
state, it's possible

1095
00:56:21,750 --> 00:56:25,650
that it actually doesn't have
that much data from where

1096
00:56:25,650 --> 00:56:27,750
it ended up after it
made this mistake.

1097
00:56:27,750 --> 00:56:29,250
Even if it was a
small mistake, it's

1098
00:56:29,250 --> 00:56:32,930
going to be potentially
underrepresented in the data.

1099
00:56:32,930 --> 00:56:35,770
And if it's underrepresented,
it's actually even more likely

1100
00:56:35,770 --> 00:56:38,270
to make a mistake in the future.

1101
00:56:38,270 --> 00:56:40,190
And so if it's more
likely to make a mistake,

1102
00:56:40,190 --> 00:56:45,170
it might actually drift away
from the distribution even more.

1103
00:56:45,170 --> 00:56:47,630
And at that point, maybe here
it's actually very far away,

1104
00:56:47,630 --> 00:56:52,210
and it makes maybe an
even bigger mistake.

1105
00:56:52,210 --> 00:56:57,290
So this is often referred
to as compounding errors.

1106
00:56:57,290 --> 00:56:59,790
Because they can start
drifting of the distribution

1107
00:56:59,790 --> 00:57:01,410
when you make mistakes,
those mistakes

1108
00:57:01,410 --> 00:57:06,050
can then compound as you
roll out your policy.

1109
00:57:06,050 --> 00:57:07,810
And as a result of this--

1110
00:57:07,810 --> 00:57:10,690
and one thing that you see
here is that the distribution

1111
00:57:10,690 --> 00:57:11,970
of states--

1112
00:57:11,970 --> 00:57:14,490
this pen, sorry.

1113
00:57:14,490 --> 00:57:20,170
The distribution of states
that are visited by your policy

1114
00:57:20,170 --> 00:57:23,200
is actually different from the
distribution of states that's

1115
00:57:23,200 --> 00:57:29,320
visited by the
expert, and this means

1116
00:57:29,320 --> 00:57:35,343
that it differs fundamentally
from supervised learning.

1117
00:57:35,343 --> 00:57:37,260
So errors can lead away
from the distribution.

1118
00:57:37,260 --> 00:57:40,140
Error can then compound.

1119
00:57:40,140 --> 00:57:41,640
The states visited
by the expert are

1120
00:57:41,640 --> 00:57:44,440
different than the states
visited by the learned policy.

1121
00:57:44,440 --> 00:57:46,820
This is referred to
as covariate shift.

1122
00:57:46,820 --> 00:57:49,960
So it's a distribution shift.

1123
00:57:49,960 --> 00:57:56,120
And that distribution shift
can lead to worse performance.

1124
00:57:56,120 --> 00:57:59,680
It also makes it different
from supervised learning.

1125
00:57:59,680 --> 00:58:03,200
So this is one of the reasons
why fitting the distribution

1126
00:58:03,200 --> 00:58:05,500
really well is very important.

1127
00:58:05,500 --> 00:58:07,080
Because if you
don't fit it well,

1128
00:58:07,080 --> 00:58:10,080
and you learn only the mean,
for example, then you're

1129
00:58:10,080 --> 00:58:13,110
even more likely to have
these compounding errors.

1130
00:58:13,110 --> 00:58:15,505

1131
00:58:15,505 --> 00:58:17,880
Even if you do fit it well,
you might still make mistakes

1132
00:58:17,880 --> 00:58:21,700
and might drift of
the distribution.

1133
00:58:21,700 --> 00:58:23,330
So how can we address this?

1134
00:58:23,330 --> 00:58:26,460

1135
00:58:26,460 --> 00:58:28,740
One solution is just to
collect a ton of data,

1136
00:58:28,740 --> 00:58:31,900
train for as long as possible to
fit the data as well as you can,

1137
00:58:31,900 --> 00:58:34,300
and hope for the best.

1138
00:58:34,300 --> 00:58:35,800
If you have the
capacity to do this,

1139
00:58:35,800 --> 00:58:38,620
it's not a terrible solution.

1140
00:58:38,620 --> 00:58:40,900
But there's also
some other solutions,

1141
00:58:40,900 --> 00:58:45,320
and these solutions are actually
quite popular in practice.

1142
00:58:45,320 --> 00:58:48,980
And basically the key
alternative solution

1143
00:58:48,980 --> 00:58:52,500
is to try to collect
corrective data.

1144
00:58:52,500 --> 00:58:55,500
And so if we're in
this scenario, what

1145
00:58:55,500 --> 00:59:01,320
we can try to do is, as the
policy starts to make a mistake,

1146
00:59:01,320 --> 00:59:03,660
we could try to say,
OK, if you're here,

1147
00:59:03,660 --> 00:59:07,500
the correct action
is to do this.

1148
00:59:07,500 --> 00:59:11,220
And this green data can
be very, very helpful

1149
00:59:11,220 --> 00:59:15,760
to allow the policy to recover
from mistakes that it made.

1150
00:59:15,760 --> 00:59:20,810

1151
00:59:20,810 --> 00:59:23,930
So the corrective data
would be, ideally, you

1152
00:59:23,930 --> 00:59:26,890
would learn something like this
that tells your policy exactly

1153
00:59:26,890 --> 00:59:28,075
how to recover.

1154
00:59:28,075 --> 00:59:29,950
And then if you collect
this corrective data,

1155
00:59:29,950 --> 00:59:33,570
then you could combine the data
in green with the data in black,

1156
00:59:33,570 --> 00:59:36,970
train a policy on
that combined data.

1157
00:59:36,970 --> 00:59:39,890
And then if it
makes a mistake, it

1158
00:59:39,890 --> 00:59:42,850
should actually have examples
of how to recover and get back

1159
00:59:42,850 --> 00:59:44,610
to the distribution
because we're actually

1160
00:59:44,610 --> 00:59:46,650
running our policy
and then getting

1161
00:59:46,650 --> 00:59:51,410
examples of how to recover
after running our policy.

1162
00:59:51,410 --> 00:59:54,648
Does this not overfit the model
and make it less generalizable?

1163
00:59:54,648 --> 00:59:57,190
You will need to make sure that
when you collect online data,

1164
00:59:57,190 --> 00:59:59,930
you collect it in diverse
scenarios and not just very

1165
00:59:59,930 --> 01:00:01,150
narrow scenarios.

1166
01:00:01,150 --> 01:00:03,670
If you only collect it in a
very small amount of scenarios,

1167
01:00:03,670 --> 01:00:05,650
then these corrections
won't be as diverse

1168
01:00:05,650 --> 01:00:07,787
as your original data set.

1169
01:00:07,787 --> 01:00:10,370
So the main difference between
the learned policy distribution

1170
01:00:10,370 --> 01:00:15,830
and the expert distribution,
it is just how the actions

1171
01:00:15,830 --> 01:00:17,540
led to particular states.

1172
01:00:17,540 --> 01:00:20,590

1173
01:00:20,590 --> 01:00:22,950
I'm using this as
shorthand for the states

1174
01:00:22,950 --> 01:00:26,630
visited by the policy.

1175
01:00:26,630 --> 01:00:30,570
And when you start to
incorporate these corrections,

1176
01:00:30,570 --> 01:00:33,510
you're expanding the
distributions of states

1177
01:00:33,510 --> 01:00:36,750
that you have data
for, and you're

1178
01:00:36,750 --> 01:00:40,030
making your
distribution of states

1179
01:00:40,030 --> 01:00:42,070
seen in your data
set wider to include

1180
01:00:42,070 --> 01:00:45,910
both p of s and the states
that your policy might

1181
01:00:45,910 --> 01:00:48,240
be likely to visit
if it makes mistakes.

1182
01:00:48,240 --> 01:00:51,550

1183
01:00:51,550 --> 01:00:54,790
So in terms of what this
looks like as an algorithm,

1184
01:00:54,790 --> 01:00:57,410
we will first roll
out our policy.

1185
01:00:57,410 --> 01:01:00,470
So this is what's
shown in blue here.

1186
01:01:00,470 --> 01:01:06,630
Then we will query an expert
at the states that you visited.

1187
01:01:06,630 --> 01:01:11,430
And these actions in green
are the same as the orange

1188
01:01:11,430 --> 01:01:14,570
a star on the slide.

1189
01:01:14,570 --> 01:01:18,370
And then we will aggregate
the corrective data

1190
01:01:18,370 --> 01:01:22,770
with the first
demonstration data set,

1191
01:01:22,770 --> 01:01:28,410
and then we'll update the policy
on the aggregated data set.

1192
01:01:28,410 --> 01:01:30,490
And this updated
policy, you could

1193
01:01:30,490 --> 01:01:34,170
fine tune the policy,
so warm start it,

1194
01:01:34,170 --> 01:01:37,130
trained on the original
data set and then update it

1195
01:01:37,130 --> 01:01:37,990
with fine tuning.

1196
01:01:37,990 --> 01:01:39,365
You could also
train from scratch

1197
01:01:39,365 --> 01:01:41,167
if you're concerned
about, I don't know,

1198
01:01:41,167 --> 01:01:43,250
some biases or something
like that, oftentimes you

1199
01:01:43,250 --> 01:01:44,913
might fine tune.

1200
01:01:44,913 --> 01:01:46,830
And then you can actually
repeat this process.

1201
01:01:46,830 --> 01:01:48,670
So you can train on
the corrective data,

1202
01:01:48,670 --> 01:01:51,250
get a better policy, and
then maybe your policy

1203
01:01:51,250 --> 01:01:54,310
actually starts making
you have another policy,

1204
01:01:54,310 --> 01:01:57,170
and maybe it makes mistakes
in a different way.

1205
01:01:57,170 --> 01:02:00,530
And in that case, you'd
want to then collect

1206
01:02:00,530 --> 01:02:03,890
some corrective data
from that second policy

1207
01:02:03,890 --> 01:02:09,090
if a new failure mode or
a new mistake emerges.

1208
01:02:09,090 --> 01:02:12,680
And so this is a
repetitive process.

1209
01:02:12,680 --> 01:02:14,992
This name has a terrible
algorithm because it's not

1210
01:02:14,992 --> 01:02:15,700
very descriptive.

1211
01:02:15,700 --> 01:02:18,760
But it's called data set
aggregation or DAgger

1212
01:02:18,760 --> 01:02:21,280
because you're aggregating data.

1213
01:02:21,280 --> 01:02:24,520
It's helpful to know
terminology, but this, I think,

1214
01:02:24,520 --> 01:02:26,360
was a bad choice.

1215
01:02:26,360 --> 01:02:29,520
And it's a data efficient
way to learn from an expert.

1216
01:02:29,520 --> 01:02:32,920
But it can be hard
to query an expert.

1217
01:02:32,920 --> 01:02:34,840
And so getting
these green actions

1218
01:02:34,840 --> 01:02:36,060
can be a little bit tricky.

1219
01:02:36,060 --> 01:02:37,920
So you might have
to actually ask

1220
01:02:37,920 --> 01:02:42,440
a person OK, this is what
my car did on the road, what

1221
01:02:42,440 --> 01:02:46,000
steering command should it have
done instead in those scenarios?

1222
01:02:46,000 --> 01:02:48,840
And so this data actually
can be potentially very

1223
01:02:48,840 --> 01:02:50,800
hard to collect.

1224
01:02:50,800 --> 01:02:53,040
So how do we get
the correct data?

1225
01:02:53,040 --> 01:02:55,800
What this would look like
is you would run your policy

1226
01:02:55,800 --> 01:02:57,880
to get this blue trajectory.

1227
01:02:57,880 --> 01:03:07,520
And so this trajectory is like
a sequence of S1, a1, S2, a2,

1228
01:03:07,520 --> 01:03:08,150
and so forth.

1229
01:03:08,150 --> 01:03:11,540

1230
01:03:11,540 --> 01:03:13,200
This is not possible
in some scenarios,

1231
01:03:13,200 --> 01:03:17,900
but you'd ask a person for this
S, what should the a have been?

1232
01:03:17,900 --> 01:03:19,947
And for this S, what
should the a have been?

1233
01:03:19,947 --> 01:03:21,780
And for this S, what
should the a have been?

1234
01:03:21,780 --> 01:03:24,060
Now, there are scenarios
where you can't do that,

1235
01:03:24,060 --> 01:03:27,420
and where this algorithm is
just not possible to run,

1236
01:03:27,420 --> 01:03:33,238
and that leads into
potentially another way

1237
01:03:33,238 --> 01:03:35,280
that we might be able to
collect corrective data.

1238
01:03:35,280 --> 01:03:37,140
We might not be able to tell
exactly that this is exactly

1239
01:03:37,140 --> 01:03:38,848
the steering command
you should have done

1240
01:03:38,848 --> 01:03:41,020
because that's very hard to do.

1241
01:03:41,020 --> 01:03:45,700
And so what we could do
instead is instead of saying,

1242
01:03:45,700 --> 01:03:49,100
for each of these
actions we want

1243
01:03:49,100 --> 01:03:50,908
to query what the
expert would have done,

1244
01:03:50,908 --> 01:03:52,700
we're actually going
to let the expert take

1245
01:03:52,700 --> 01:03:54,800
full control at some point.

1246
01:03:54,800 --> 01:03:56,740
And so what this would
look like is something

1247
01:03:56,740 --> 01:03:58,240
where you start drifting off.

1248
01:03:58,240 --> 01:04:02,220
The expert notices that a
mistake was made, and then takes

1249
01:04:02,220 --> 01:04:05,860
full control, for example,
starts driving the car

1250
01:04:05,860 --> 01:04:09,366
rather than letting the
policy drive the car.

1251
01:04:09,366 --> 01:04:11,130
And then in this
case, you can then

1252
01:04:11,130 --> 01:04:15,770
add those partial
demonstrations to your data

1253
01:04:15,770 --> 01:04:20,570
set instead of trying to query
the expert on the policy states.

1254
01:04:20,570 --> 01:04:24,130
And so here you would start to
roll out the learned policy.

1255
01:04:24,130 --> 01:04:27,890
The expert intervenes when
the policy makes a mistake.

1256
01:04:27,890 --> 01:04:31,330
The expert provides a partial
demonstration from the state

1257
01:04:31,330 --> 01:04:34,850
that the policy visited, so
it would take full control

1258
01:04:34,850 --> 01:04:37,930
and then aggregate this
partial demonstrations

1259
01:04:37,930 --> 01:04:42,910
with the original data set in
order to then update the policy.

1260
01:04:42,910 --> 01:04:45,088
And again, you can
repeat this process

1261
01:04:45,088 --> 01:04:47,130
so that you can cover
different potential failure

1262
01:04:47,130 --> 01:04:50,050
modes of the policy.

1263
01:04:50,050 --> 01:04:53,090
In all of these cases, the
best performance of the policy

1264
01:04:53,090 --> 01:04:55,690
is upper bounded by the expert.

1265
01:04:55,690 --> 01:04:57,890
The question was, if
we're detecting a mistake,

1266
01:04:57,890 --> 01:04:59,890
is that mostly if you've
collided with something

1267
01:04:59,890 --> 01:05:01,330
or something else?

1268
01:05:01,330 --> 01:05:07,236
This is something
that's subjective and is

1269
01:05:07,236 --> 01:05:10,830
up to the demonstrator
in many ways.

1270
01:05:10,830 --> 01:05:13,670
Oftentimes you want to
be a bit more proactive.

1271
01:05:13,670 --> 01:05:15,690
And before the car
hits something,

1272
01:05:15,690 --> 01:05:16,995
you would want to take control.

1273
01:05:16,995 --> 01:05:18,870
And so one of the
downsides of this algorithm

1274
01:05:18,870 --> 01:05:21,410
is that it can sometimes be
hard to catch mistakes quickly.

1275
01:05:21,410 --> 01:05:23,790
Or if the policy decides
to, I don't know,

1276
01:05:23,790 --> 01:05:27,070
drastically change the action
and it was unpredictable,

1277
01:05:27,070 --> 01:05:30,790
that can be very hard
to proactively prevent.

1278
01:05:30,790 --> 01:05:32,710
And you want ideally
this to be in a scenario

1279
01:05:32,710 --> 01:05:35,110
where you can predict.

1280
01:05:35,110 --> 01:05:37,870
If the policy is starting
to drift into another lane,

1281
01:05:37,870 --> 01:05:40,990
re centering it,
that sort of thing.

1282
01:05:40,990 --> 01:05:43,030
This is actually
something that companies

1283
01:05:43,030 --> 01:05:45,250
do in driving where
they let the policy run.

1284
01:05:45,250 --> 01:05:46,610
They have a safety driver.

1285
01:05:46,610 --> 01:05:48,850
And then the safety driver
will take full control

1286
01:05:48,850 --> 01:05:51,610
if it seems like the policy
either doesn't know what to do,

1287
01:05:51,610 --> 01:05:56,187
and it's like stalling, or it's
starting to make a mistake.

1288
01:05:56,187 --> 01:05:57,770
Now, We've seen
failure modes of that.

1289
01:05:57,770 --> 01:05:59,687
We've seen cases where
safety drivers were not

1290
01:05:59,687 --> 01:06:04,230
able to prevent a mistake
that was more major.

1291
01:06:04,230 --> 01:06:09,100
And so there's downsides
relying on the demonstrator

1292
01:06:09,100 --> 01:06:13,820
to proactively prevent a
huge mistake from happening.

1293
01:06:13,820 --> 01:06:17,780
But it is a practical interface
for providing corrections

1294
01:06:17,780 --> 01:06:21,420
in a lot of scenarios, and
can help improve performance

1295
01:06:21,420 --> 01:06:23,660
significantly.

1296
01:06:23,660 --> 01:06:25,460
So the question was,
can you explain again

1297
01:06:25,460 --> 01:06:27,960
why if you're correcting
a narrow corrective data,

1298
01:06:27,960 --> 01:06:30,260
why that would lead
to overfitting?

1299
01:06:30,260 --> 01:06:32,480
So let's take the
driving example.

1300
01:06:32,480 --> 01:06:36,480
If your original driving data
was all over the United States,

1301
01:06:36,480 --> 01:06:38,740
and then your corrective
driving data was only

1302
01:06:38,740 --> 01:06:43,420
on Stanford's campus, then
when you combine the data set,

1303
01:06:43,420 --> 01:06:46,300
it might only learn
that corrective behavior

1304
01:06:46,300 --> 01:06:48,040
in a more narrow
set of scenarios,

1305
01:06:48,040 --> 01:06:50,660
and might also overfit
to the Stanford data,

1306
01:06:50,660 --> 01:06:53,680
leading to a policy that is
perhaps less generalizable.

1307
01:06:53,680 --> 01:06:56,400
And ideally, when you
collected corrective data,

1308
01:06:56,400 --> 01:07:00,410
it would be in a broader set
of scenarios than campus.

1309
01:07:00,410 --> 01:07:05,000

